            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Adds up the area under a curve given as samples: the integral. Between each pair of neighbouring samples it takes the area of the trapezoid they make - their average times the spacing - and sums them.\n\nFrom speeds it gives the distance travelled; from a joint's power, the work done; from a rate, the total; from a level over time, the overall dose. It is more accurate than adding the samples and multiplying by the spacing (t.sum), which counts each end as if it had lasted a whole step.\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.trapezoid <spacing: float>\nDefault: spacing 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.trapezoid 0.01667\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe samples (a list or tensor). Every input is integrated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between samples - the time between frames (1/60 for 60 frames a second), so the answer comes out per second rather than per frame.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to integrate: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narea:\nThe total area: one number, or one per row or column.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA speed rising steadily from 0 to 4 metres a second, sampled every half second. The distance covered is the area of a triangle, 2 seconds by 4 metres a second, halved: 4 metres, which t.trapezoid gets exactly. t.sum times the spacing gives 5, counting the final speed of 4 as if it had lasted a whole half second.\n\nRELATED:\nt.cumulative_trapezoid gives the running total at every sample. t.cumsum is the running sum without the averaging. t.gradient goes the other way, from a total to its rate. np.trapezoid is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds up the area under a curve given as samples: the integral. Between each pair of neighbouring samples it takes the area of the trapezoid they make - their average times the spacing - and sums them.\n\nFrom speeds it gives the distance travelled; from a joint's power, the work done; from a rate, the total; from a level over time, the overall dose. It is more accurate than adding the samples and multiplying by the spacing (t.sum), which counts each end as if it had lasted a whole step.\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.trapezoid <spacing: float>\nDefault: spacing 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.trapezoid 0.01667\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe samples (a list or tensor). Every input is integrated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between samples - the time between frames (1/60 for 60 frames a second), so the answer comes out per second rather than per frame.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to integrate: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narea:\nThe total area: one number, or one per row or column.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA speed rising steadily from 0 to 4 metres a second, sampled every half second. The distance covered is the area of a triangle, 2 seconds by 4 metres a second, halved: 4 metres, which t.trapezoid gets exactly. t.sum times the spacing gives 5, counting the final speed of 4 as if it had lasted a whole half second.\n\nRELATED:\nt.cumulative_trapezoid gives the running total at every sample. t.cumsum is the running sum without the averaging. t.gradient goes the other way, from a total to its rate. np.trapezoid is the numpy version."
        },
